IOP emulator: host SPU2 hook (T-302)

What it adds: an interface, ps2xIOP/include/ps2x/iop/iop_host_spu2.h (iopSetHostSpu2), with which the host
program installs an SPU2 on the IOP emulator. With one installed:
- every access to the SPU2 registers (IOP 0x1F900000-0x1F9007FF) goes to the host's read16/write16 (a 32-bit access
  as two 16-bit ones, low half first; an 8-bit write as read-modify-write of the 16-bit register);
- a start of IOP DMA channel 4 (SPU2 core 0) or 7 (core 1) hands the bytes (address, block size and block count
  from MADR/BCR; direction from CHCR bit 0) to the host's dma_write/dma_read, and CHCR.TR stays set until the host
  reports the end of that core's DMA; then TR is cleared, MADR is moved to the end and the channel's interrupt
  (0x24 / 0x28) is delivered. The SPU2 interrupt event delivers IOP interrupt line 9;
- every execution slice of runEeCycles first advances the host SPU2 by the IOP cycles elapsed and drains its events.
  One emulator cycle is one cycle of the 36.864 MHz IOP clock (1 instruction or kernel call = 1 cycle, EE cycles / 8),
  the unit the host SPU2 counts, so no conversion is needed. While every thread sleeps the idle skip is limited to
  768 * 32 cycles so that SPU2 events are not delivered late;
- the heuristic HLE libsd service (the one that decodes VAG samples itself) is inactive, so the samples are not
  played twice. The registers' old storage and the faked "DMA complete" flag are bypassed for the SPU2 window.
Without a host SPU2 installed nothing changes (the old storage, the faked DMA-complete flag and interrupt, the HLE
service).

How it was verified: tests/runtime/iop_host_spu2_test.cpp (build with tests/runtime/CMakeLists.txt): a fake SPU2
records every call, a small IOP program built in memory touches the registers in every width and starts a DMA on
channel 4 and on channel 7; the test checks the calls and bytes, that each channel stays busy until its own
event, the interrupts, the MADR, the cycle total, and the unchanged behaviour with no host installed.

Based on: the Killzone recompilation's patches/ps2recomp/0006-spu2-host-audio.patch, repository
https://github.com/drakolordx7/KillzoneRecomp, commit 5f0700079f9ac4e14799d66d01747dbcdcec55f6 (GPL-3.0). The
shape of the hook (register routing in IopMemory, DMA start in the CHCR write, completion by an event, advancing the
SPU2 in runCycles, the idle cap) follows it; the interface (a user pointer and the callbacks of don_spu2.h, events
polled instead of pushed), the cycle bookkeeping, the byte accesses and the HLE service switch are new. Its other
changes (sceSifGetOtherData, WaitEventFlag outside a thread, the time-ahead callback nextEvent) are not part of
this patch.

diff --git a/ps2xIOP/include/ps2x/iop/iop_host_spu2.h b/ps2xIOP/include/ps2x/iop/iop_host_spu2.h
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..9d58326
--- /dev/null
+++ b/ps2xIOP/include/ps2x/iop/iop_host_spu2.h
@@ -0,0 +1,73 @@
+#pragma once
+
+#include <cstdint>
+
+// Optional host SPU2. The host installs it once, before the IOP runs, and from then on the IOP emulator routes
+// to it (a "host SPU2"; without one nothing below applies and the built-in stand-ins behave exactly as before):
+//
+//  - Registers. Every access of IOP code to IOP physical 0x1F900000-0x1F9007FF (any mirror of it: the top three
+//    address bits are ignored) goes to read16/write16 with the address of the 16-bit register. A 32-bit access
+//    becomes two 16-bit ones, low halfword first (the order the bus delivers them). An 8-bit read returns the
+//    chosen byte of read16; an 8-bit write is a read16, a byte merge and a write16 of that register. Addresses
+//    that are not 2-byte aligned for a 16-bit access, or 4-byte aligned for a 32-bit one, are split into bytes.
+//    The rest of the 0x1F900800-0x1F9FFFFF window keeps the old storage.
+//  - DMA. A write of CHCR with the TR bit (bit 24) set on IOP DMA channel 4 (SPU2 core 0: MADR 0x1F8010C0,
+//    BCR 0x1F8010C4, CHCR 0x1F8010C8) or channel 7 (core 1: 0x1F801500, 0x1F801504, 0x1F801508) starts a
+//    transfer of block size (BCR bits 0-15, in words) times block count (BCR bits 16-31) words, 4 bytes each,
+//    from/to the IOP RAM at MADR. CHCR bit 0 set = IOP RAM to SPU2 (dma_write, the bytes are handed over before
+//    the call returns); clear = SPU2 to IOP RAM (dma_read fills a buffer that is then copied to IOP RAM). TR
+//    stays set, the channel is busy, until poll_event reports the end of that core's DMA. Then TR is cleared,
+//    MADR is left at the end address and the channel's interrupt (0x24 for channel 4, 0x28 for channel 7) is
+//    delivered. If the guest cleared TR itself in the meantime the end event is dropped (no interrupt).
+//  - Interrupt. The SPU2 interrupt event delivers IOP interrupt line 9 (the handler registered with intrman
+//    RegisterIntrHandler(9, ...) and enabled with EnableIntr(9)).
+//  - Time. IOP time is counted in cycles of the 36,864,000 Hz IOP clock: one executed instruction (or one
+//    imported kernel call) is one cycle, idle time skipped while every thread sleeps counts as it passes, and the
+//    EE cycles given to runEeCycles convert at 8 EE cycles = 1 IOP cycle (294,912,000 Hz / 8). So the emulator's
+//    cycle counter already is the unit don_spu2_advance wants and nothing is converted. Before each execution
+//    slice and after the last one of runEeCycles, advance(delta) passes the cycles elapsed since the previous
+//    call (their sum equals the emulator's cycle counter since the last reset), then poll_event is called until
+//    it returns 0 and the events are delivered. A register access therefore sees the SPU2 up to one slice (256
+//    cycles) behind the IOP. While every thread sleeps, the emulator skips ahead at most max_idle_cycles at a
+//    time so that SPU2 events are not delivered late by a long idle jump.
+//
+// Units and meanings match src/spu2bridge/don_spu2.h of this repository, so the glue is one line per callback
+// (type: 1 = SPU2 interrupt, 4 = end of the core 0 DMA, 7 = end of the core 1 DMA).
+//
+// Threading: all callbacks are called from the thread that runs the IOP. Install and remove from that thread
+// while the IOP is not running. One host SPU2 per process.
+namespace ps2x::iop
+{
+    inline constexpr int kHostSpu2EventIrq = 1;
+    inline constexpr int kHostSpu2EventDmaEnd0 = 4;
+    inline constexpr int kHostSpu2EventDmaEnd1 = 7;
+
+    struct IopHostSpu2
+    {
+        void *user = nullptr; // passed as the first argument of every callback
+
+        // Required. A host SPU2 is installed only if all six are set.
+        void (*write16)(void *user, uint32_t addr, uint16_t value) = nullptr;
+        uint16_t (*read16)(void *user, uint32_t addr) = nullptr;
+        // data points into IOP RAM (valid only during the call); bytes is the transfer size (a multiple of 4,
+        // clamped to the end of IOP RAM). Returns are ignored.
+        void (*dma_write)(void *user, int core, const uint8_t *data, uint32_t bytes) = nullptr;
+        // dst is a scratch buffer of `bytes` bytes that the callee fills.
+        void (*dma_read)(void *user, int core, uint8_t *dst, uint32_t bytes) = nullptr;
+        void (*advance)(void *user, uint64_t iop_cycles) = nullptr;
+        // Returns 1 and fills *type and *iop_cycle (either may be ignored) if an event is queued, else 0.
+        int (*poll_event)(void *user, int *type, uint64_t *iop_cycle) = nullptr;
+
+        // Optional. Called when the IOP emulator is reset (module state, RAM and cycle counter go back to 0).
+        void (*reset)(void *user) = nullptr;
+
+        // Longest idle skip, in IOP cycles, while the host SPU2 is installed. 0 = the default (768 * 32, 32
+        // audio frames, about 0.67 ms).
+        uint64_t max_idle_cycles = 0;
+    };
+
+    // Installs a copy of *hooks, or removes the host SPU2 if hooks is null or incomplete (see above).
+    void iopSetHostSpu2(const IopHostSpu2 *hooks);
+    // The installed host SPU2, or null.
+    const IopHostSpu2 *iopHostSpu2();
+}
